#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198082 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "eae94514-cd92-5405-90b1-ee09dad18e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-13162" :question/problem "Sketch a representation of the set $S = \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{N}$ in the Cartesian plane." :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS = \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{N}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS=\n\\Big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z,\\, y\\in \\mathbb N\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_1\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any integer number and whose $y$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}{1}}.$\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\Big\\{ (x, {\\color{red}{1}}) \\: : \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_1$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/6b63e973bbcc3e25d601a1f6cef97f5a00cc22e738c46d04a1ac06851421894e.png)\n\nNext, consider $S_2\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any integer number and whose $y$-coordinate equals ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\Big\\{ (x,{\\color{blue}{2}}) \\: : \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_2$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/f24ce8f7610d096c795a8a74b0b714b720c36442042c28b66432d6b2102fb9d4.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $S_3,S_4,S_5,\\ldots,$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup {S}_{4}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{N}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/c999c61d8ffbb27d47272811e32340cef3253b4730e684be10615487a8b27200.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198097 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "bb40c174-3739-50df-835e-5d47c9902d5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13192" :question/problem "Sketch a representation of the set $S = \\{1, 2\\} \\times (0, 1)$ in the Cartesian plane." :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS = \\{1,2\\} \\times (0,1)\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $1$ or $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\nS=\n\\Big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in \\{{\\color{red}{1}},{\\color{blue}{2}}\\},\\, y\\in (0,1) \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_1\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}{1}}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\Big\\{ ({\\color{red}{1}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in(0,1) \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/56f200fce557749a56b9b5b51bc670da5f8c7f2cdb0196392ee80efcd6be980a.png)\n\nNext, consider $S_2\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1).$\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\big\\{ ({\\color{blue}{2}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in(0,1) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/f96074cc265bba6eb59717364d2aa5390fdccea4d398fe5ae0b99f7ad82f0b93.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS = S_1\\cup S_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/0c0529aaa680b4b572d17f2cfbfb76d805134e72c50ab3ed469286cc025185a3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198133 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "aa337681-5d8c-5ea1-b0fe-5d8cd15040a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13320" :question/problem "Sketch a representation of the set $\\,\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane given that $I = [-1,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = \\{ i \\} \\times [0, \\, i^2].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x \\: : \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\nX_{-0.5} = \\{ -0.5 \\} \\times [0, 0.25] = \\big\\{ (-0.5,k) \\: : \\: k \\in [0, 0.25] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5, 0)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.25)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/ccc75eac899efcab1d0b6fde12609a3ea8f61d6fec74c739ef3393fad32645a9.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = \\{ i \\} \\times [0, i^2] = \\big\\{ (i,k) \\: : \\: k \\in [0, i^2] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i, i^2).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/60c98be5d97748e9285bb5e9c30bba9781a5ef99245789718a7828b18a6df8fb.png)\n\n \n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union $\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/b3a61d686c78c76a4f461c1c771437d31030339b634661da8b8dc4f492db55eb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198134 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "67569060-2f33-5a8d-ae73-690742d3d318" :question/math-academy-id "e-13321" :question/problem "Let $I = [0,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1].$ Sketch a representation of $\\displaystyle \\bigcap_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x \\: : \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for all}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\nX_{0.5} = [-0.5,0] \\times [-1, 1] = \\big\\{ (a,b) \\: : \\: a \\in [-0.5, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(-0.5, -1),$ $(-0.5, 1),$ $(0, 1),$ and $(0, -1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8ad650cb3f2fa4da920a442e8cc1b24af5027595cba4e43138eff2126cf4c102.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1] = \\big\\{ (a,b) \\: : \\: a \\in [-i, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(-i,-1),$ $(-i,1),$ $(0, 1),$ and $(0, -1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/a37cfa508cf3a60536084fbc1e57c5aeac3414904d2ad72ef1deda85dc4eddd8.png)\n\n \n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $\\{0\\}\\times [-1,1]{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/5e9a611629f4370c57e77a057439c759670afa17c14c4b2339e1d471f616ea72.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "132f77bd-9ddf-5caf-96a1-e220936f45d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307934} {:answer/id #uuid "098babed-40a5-5df9-8de1-0a88c19800f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/df4a1ecffe6302ee57250944ec8f178f46a066b2edff2ed1afb200e7b0217cf7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307935} {:answer/id #uuid "715d6dad-5b8d-5a07-ab6f-cc9c2db51162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/6b5b39546eabacdf948bd87e793727fdc8cd8f2664df037be53bfb630fc41670.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307936} {:answer/id #uuid "280a367a-db86-510d-83e6-8f1354018cdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/506d86848903de9f038d541152aabf67d99d0f4d0a7e5f5772b811ff1cf9dade.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307937} {:answer/id #uuid "51ec42ac-80bd-51b7-9c6b-1a00dd8a1002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/17a176a0e7cb254483d93741cd2c1e3db581005f479928ac36809e55bc970c07.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "132f77bd-9ddf-5caf-96a1-e220936f45d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307934} :answer-field/id #uuid "dda8c1e1-bad9-5b1c-b928-a0d2962fffd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30b7b0c8-79fe-53ad-bb30-601d2f7e078d" :question/math-academy-id "q-172690" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}={1}\\times [0,1]={(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/71dd97607df48caf579c2e476520507ede035f65c336064f022cd900b6c120fc.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,i]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4b87c830620f54e5b3a3bb7923be971b4d9d4abb98cfb3dcdc8387f376a0b452.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6e36dc9-ca9d-5871-ac65-5a8e2fd56e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/a2786a8cfb41d8fb352c95dc36688fb2eff4773680be54be9dd3d1c696ae588a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307941} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb187e0-61a4-5b94-8c71-8517589c46d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307942} {:answer/id #uuid "46ae9087-5cf5-516b-81da-46b3beae2ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4fa495b2b7e5528f5aa70abe8ce778fc0c1e7d5bcd4a9b855a72ee1469ed5f01.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307943} {:answer/id #uuid "3452be50-9b3a-545e-9265-22146b078969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/3b3d7d98bdc1c336c9151bff06f7d07bea7e2228efb253b0fc16cdd2de1faa99.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307944} {:answer/id #uuid "1e664514-cba7-564e-8f55-c3cbd248245f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/e5ed1251cb899e92d9f2e7dd6111140b07556432b5240fa5a9efacd33ce19989.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfb187e0-61a4-5b94-8c71-8517589c46d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307942} :answer-field/id #uuid "dead5edd-27f4-5ebd-8ce8-6749e3e0cbe8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "280b10a4-5dc0-5bb2-b73b-6d6eeefafeca" :question/math-academy-id "q-175295" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times {-i}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=[0,1]\\times {-1}={(k,-1)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(1,-1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/a39443fd1b3764ff60c0c052085134ad7eeda6759c07c4d936fc03cd121f72dd.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times {-i}={(k,-i)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(0,-i)$ to the point $(i,-i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/7f18af14c55acc4bc2cd63b601dba0bde3cd51c0284b398b401c854537003926.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "284a26e8-834e-59ba-a96d-b6c8e7b0d6cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307948} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f6775e-125a-5c36-ada2-acecc7bc0269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/71e38fd694074e4c8d778ad7428504ce4ebde9304a7e9c11183bd0b4c8c0ce90.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307949} {:answer/id #uuid "abcd9f4b-1903-5673-80e9-eabe64cc47c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/0a3bb1509f0c861b7eb5b2718f51719b3b2052ffbddb089922c6b2d84e365586.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307950} {:answer/id #uuid "4a094785-f935-5f53-a566-3ff0e4f0eaff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/ab2520b1ee68acbd46dc0984264bdbb9107e9ddd84a9666ba38770aabc31bb9b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307951} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5a2d4d-e28d-521e-867c-0d244f5b21b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/21def8c03fa64fa3e24d010489284a13786c3f237271228ac16f6226ecf57fa5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "284a26e8-834e-59ba-a96d-b6c8e7b0d6cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅,$ the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307948} :answer-field/id #uuid "b030a615-15ab-506a-9f98-54dc88a88e16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccdf2b91-4206-5a3f-b15f-21feb13a3062" :question/math-academy-id "q-175302" :question/problem "Let $I=[1,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,2-i]\\times {i}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=[0,2-1]\\times {1}=[0,1]\\times {1}={(k,1)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/ddb7039dfcdbc2ef4070ce732c635a48194b6675cc3c78de6267e656c5e8387a.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,2-i]\\times {i}={(k,i)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,2-i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(0,i)$ to the point $(2-i,i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/e83644a2f0de96ee58ea893028bb7a42681ee71a15d194f2b344c4c15f60fafe.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd15d26e-bdf3-5fe0-84b9-980b25f01709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8ce0c60aafe1a41b372c1f1477241c584d6f8ae224113c360576feb37fc25a8c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307955} {:answer/id #uuid "129832fc-dc2b-57ac-840e-63de2e90981a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/fcda8aaa1da0ee1e9fee4957246497a927b4373c51d8a46db429540ea51f5083.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307956} {:answer/id #uuid "1231c5af-cdb2-54d0-952b-de9c2a8060ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/cd35c57fa9c6ce3ae2ee5961b8ae3e7d16ea1a2aa44c302a9e40f19b6c1b11ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307957} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fe220a-a262-58d6-ac9b-4071188d639e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/b6518f0f49f1147742d621fa940d49f84e7cce9c17d071fdfeb4a2e96abbf591.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307958} {:answer/id #uuid "d217b59c-798f-51ff-abfb-5b1969eb6aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/310ba014b846afb8d4f2bff610e762da71b47e8c4daa2d6ffebf4b62ecc4d1db.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd15d26e-bdf3-5fe0-84b9-980b25f01709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8ce0c60aafe1a41b372c1f1477241c584d6f8ae224113c360576feb37fc25a8c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307955} :answer-field/id #uuid "39d5bd33-5d17-5469-8cbe-cdc0b0c554f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15afbaad-5b0e-58d9-8df7-ceeef530333d" :question/math-academy-id "q-177652" :question/problem "Let $I=[-1,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [-|i|,|i|].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{-0.5}={-0.5}\\times [-0.5,0.5]={(-0.5,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5,-0.5)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/b7793d85e02ccd05c55c34971c90be087d58274eabd21f46fe09f08ccd60797b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [-|i|,|i|]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-|i|,|i|]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,-|i|)$ to the point $(i,|i|).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/c30712295adfe51c655662cc09f8f1e23b3ea97573693154e0e0b6ba48e15c07.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8ce0c60aafe1a41b372c1f1477241c584d6f8ae224113c360576feb37fc25a8c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "957afc7c-bb56-510f-8194-063b82cf9c14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181303-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195212} {:answer/id #uuid "9d5bc49b-44d8-5eec-82fd-fbfe70cdcd9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181303-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195213} {:answer/id #uuid "2b692d7e-4b16-534a-b4c7-20d13f0fff03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181303-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195214} {:answer/id #uuid "5a90b893-f6a6-5139-be4d-13159501eb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181303-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195215} {:answer/id #uuid "785b2895-4c8b-5959-b6c8-78b4beeb915f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181303-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "785b2895-4c8b-5959-b6c8-78b4beeb915f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181303-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195216} :answer-field/id #uuid "69d9e92f-711c-5cfe-be85-f01c136b4027" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2bbd8412-3c29-5dd3-ab23-10004aeed05b" :question/math-academy-id "q-181303" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=R\\times N$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times N\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in N}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/f8808e4c35775c754dab0bf328a8d129e423009e18ec5f4b962461eba2afcea2.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $2.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(x,2)\\,:\\,x\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/a51fe9dc5bf7c55b01f72a45d4b88bde4653f0d3da85c1231b0d54c76a6e8c81.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times N$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/7ae3c488e60d3dcda304649e575a7d2efc54be7262a54d0455550af420c39b68.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311786a8-d33e-5f52-9723-a4f227c48979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307962} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ab78d0-6e94-562a-bcc9-1e735e554629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/95c7144f229cc34fcb98b1c1fa8bda8af8ca9dff68c7ae466e388ce3e525bc85.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307963} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae879f0-d017-5959-98dd-83863794ae80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307964} {:answer/id #uuid "393ac199-32ce-5cdd-a7d3-8b36e3cc1bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/43f665cebf4c3af1f1178d94b4ceec5d60bcfd4e61ebf955ed43cb603f8232b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307965} {:answer/id #uuid "c10aba9d-99f6-59ee-8505-e2aabf77a730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/eceb9c773d52b8bae73837252a885258b5a9b735cf3f24954b8755049d7cf07d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "311786a8-d33e-5f52-9723-a4f227c48979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307962} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddff1d60-96cd-5249-ac1f-abfac8452187" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff89cd3b-d498-586f-b4f1-6477eb383eeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-181770" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=Z\\times R$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times R\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/3fdfed680e3fb46adbe8a70aac86682aacb85d43e0675923f87658635fc89a1e.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/7fbda500ecca7e18c9db9939ad78d4dcc6bf488bc7fc4de1137e764ce21a0254.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times R$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95fdd016-0bad-5595-b4a6-0749069f92b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307969} {:answer/id #uuid "a0af478e-12ba-5c4d-bde3-b3f6ca1ed4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/664f327f59e76cb22e1b9bd0a83f84c24e75ad6a0d176532437f629bf80bfc29.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307970} {:answer/id #uuid "b320fcd3-9039-5aa1-b7d8-01e0cf9387c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307971} {:answer/id #uuid "b44fbb1b-82be-5d2a-a104-74bf816fc6af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4ddec65745de14217112f6365d9dd372df5a24a13ef8bedc8db592c9ee18cab1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307972} {:answer/id #uuid "cea7954c-3904-52ec-9cb4-2bf067d4efe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/05323cccad