:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06dc01d3-636a-5a37-9129-21c0ede89387" :question/math-academy-id "q-181986" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=R\\times [-1,1]$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=R\\times [-1,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in [-1,1]}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}={(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,1]}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/588f2574b1aaf0e96895816ca34bd9c6900a9b13342f44bd4104ad2bf4270bf5.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in R$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}={(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,1]}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in R,$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $(i,-1)$ to the point $(i,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/8458978c2766d899b03149de53739650932875d6463c25dd3bbde99bc6a71ea7.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $R:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in R}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nAs $i$ runs over $R,$ these line segments sweep the following bar that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in R}{S}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/6fc81e487503ce86d3bf5366e57db087484e8bd1e8bafd52738c65e8e173c387.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db693b49-2bec-59ba-91d8-3f26a5d1d9dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195461} {:answer/id #uuid "123dada4-b1fa-58e4-9523-a85c56398af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195462} {:answer/id #uuid "34a7d35a-8582-569d-a279-cf93880bbbc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195463} {:answer/id #uuid "39ef5d1e-89e9-50ad-b865-5cb8beed15b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195464} {:answer/id #uuid "611e76b4-a957-5ff1-8317-0bab4ca89c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "123dada4-b1fa-58e4-9523-a85c56398af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-181987-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195462} :answer-field/id #uuid "35fa6e74-a2da-5824-8c02-9458dddb39ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bd0f51c-7480-5612-9de3-790f45702d19" :question/math-academy-id "q-181987" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={1,2}\\times (-1,1)$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={1,2}\\times (-1,1)\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $1$ or $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {1,2},\\,y\\in (-1,1)}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (-1,1)}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/11522acdfeaad022847fedbcf6052424216587f99f2691e80ed96152f30818a4.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in (-1,1)}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/90b6a9c00d366e4a03c8c974476fdf06bc9fa73c9c26e9a0cd6ba9a4bda8e66d.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/bbf0d5ecc57bc30c7a3f4db7a1d30b27633e4586b2768e315715df72ce41333f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a08e0f3-bb8d-5079-be43-418d1ccc80d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/9a87c5ea8ceaa8a64e30b6375a8bcaa58182ac2e97d68b44c70a0a964c4727dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308004} {:answer/id #uuid "be7e3c12-61b3-55f0-8a3e-cf088ff42f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/3274c0747b72b6b8ce457f20cd89d0f7d951c8a3bd138c9061448c1bedb42b55.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308005} {:answer/id #uuid "44d8cc1d-374d-5e3f-8099-ca10c1b15570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/824d176d4d041be340eb786ff53f8b5a352edca3ad928fc593b9799f2cc4008b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308006} {:answer/id #uuid "c59f3db3-c101-53e8-b95d-e391b9a3b0a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/b676b2201a4fe4d57cb005eb9527f429068eae4d18c55496ebabf9390fc19219.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308007} {:answer/id #uuid "4942fa94-f3a0-59c5-992e-19f4502ecfd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/340f534cb7548f2e5fcffb6153d45fd327b414aa17247bcde00b5396d24830a4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a08e0f3-bb8d-5079-be43-418d1ccc80d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/9a87c5ea8ceaa8a64e30b6375a8bcaa58182ac2e97d68b44c70a0a964c4727dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308004} :answer-field/id #uuid "177d9ab5-b029-5f42-8f69-a6b37e397e45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b944d1d3-a1d9-5050-b47a-9348621b0729" :question/math-academy-id "q-182001" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=Z\\times [-1,0]$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times [-1,0]\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a integer number and whose $y$-coordinate belongs to $[-1,0].$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in [-1,0]}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is in $[-1,0].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,0]}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/63199d9f35c331339e97ad3b2094a520d1fb6a9892bd0beac83538c43b9e2748.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is in $[-1,0].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/03a6a90b3eff572ceed341de55a0d5e625e338ca18f2ae8ee85eed3bb5278167.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times [-1,0]$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/9a87c5ea8ceaa8a64e30b6375a8bcaa58182ac2e97d68b44c70a0a964c4727dd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd71f733-0803-5a3d-8ca8-bee7f61ccb10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/54fd8fa56f44e0a4c0e3c162d6262afab99c61b565ae2bc5b2e7bcf2f9295137.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308011} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc6b10f-0e09-54c1-a8c4-f362210ff545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/5dd3ff71d17188adaa68632b6d16aed1f41e1975ec4ddd321a8160fabb0e16b8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308012} {:answer/id #uuid "effe3780-bd59-557b-8c8c-ae7aa65350b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/c454c6108cb57c16ff806fb92cd95cb4b97e8077e1038bfb3164ac1d3666f761.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308013} {:answer/id #uuid "919d0d71-daa3-5ce9-b175-2c3a5ea463b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/12b910d75a864171898a8ee8874245a684e8c943efb27c9b755c5d31ef322af7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308014} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0499e9-3170-5512-9b81-54095c32049d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/e75d513154aa16dc2ef43b24a03f8375a96ed52edd1c43d8a59798674c17585b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fc6b10f-0e09-54c1-a8c4-f362210ff545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/5dd3ff71d17188adaa68632b6d16aed1f41e1975ec4ddd321a8160fabb0e16b8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308012} :answer-field/id #uuid "baa447c1-1bf7-575e-a371-16168b0de2ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9d0afdc-f76c-528e-8547-9d5a9ac36d66" :question/math-academy-id "q-184488" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={-2,2}\\times {-1,0,1}$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing ${-2,2}\\times {-1,0,1}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS={(-2,-1),(-2,0),(-2,1),(2,-1),(2,0),(2,1)}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/5dd3ff71d17188adaa68632b6d16aed1f41e1975ec4ddd321a8160fabb0e16b8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed00671f-39a8-5ef5-acae-7a418bfae476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/30f5c0611fe32d0bd14d838bcb45a02d468b7f7ef5e37a5c48ecd7648b306fbd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308018} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd104da-d32f-5629-8097-71eb5656acf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/2d72a5616d611fa8e437fbf5f689e0b1209c8b77b0c2fb92f72c6388d79faa60.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308019} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c43e22-76aa-5964-9101-227e8b59cc95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/d19ecceb011b54492d3ea9f0d262de1ba96e21c3423ed8b083d44551a6420db9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308020} {:answer/id #uuid "55bf2f9f-11c6-5e73-847b-42e36eb055f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/7c9ed47fedfe4a23ceb80847ac7a4805afecd1c9ff9028b4800b71e0870bef75.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308021} {:answer/id #uuid "d3632e7d-cf62-5316-9d9d-cdf9a2858da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/d58cfce54cdf98d4ea8661ba91353ad5748b715d61d7f666db27a5cdef2d2431.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1c43e22-76aa-5964-9101-227e8b59cc95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/d19ecceb011b54492d3ea9f0d262de1ba96e21c3423ed8b083d44551a6420db9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308020} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcc71534-ffab-5f5b-9599-1e15722d52b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "475f1c7e-a5b8-58e4-b569-fdfc8a373029" :question/math-academy-id "q-184492" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={-1,3}\\times [-2,1]$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={-1,3}\\times [2,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $-1$ or $3$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-2,1].$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {-1,3},\\,y\\in [-2,1]}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-2,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-2,1]}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/57cb74832a9766c60819bdcc0c88290c4a05556b673965973e161895de2f530a.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $3$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-2,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(3,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-2,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/9f3d0beca2ed9eb30eb6456b67d5f17002726e9b9de6e8ed3c79d72f43bcf3ae.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/d19ecceb011b54492d3ea9f0d262de1ba96e21c3423ed8b083d44551a6420db9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f289f107-5fb9-5b10-8437-0bf19c164b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/119379ee56b15559073a7fefc9100dc8145f376c28271155fc10d9bac7a7594f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308025} {:answer/id #uuid "79538ec7-2512-5353-8259-dce1409b60a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/714c5db9aceb568a4988ce38a32e73a186178939fe0ba48cd384b3cf88f0e937.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308026} {:answer/id #uuid "5b3a0ae3-c935-558c-a2c5-0ecef7b8a1bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308027} {:answer/id #uuid "4a1272dc-fc2e-521d-ada1-2dba633e34fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/f6d06dbdcf69c4dba1d0375bbe204151c7a5d8098f7385220f3c66f35f7fa0a8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308028} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef195df-417d-5787-a1ce-4115b3fafc2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/c9ba946ca63983aece16f5243680fe8cc7dddf930f4d284fd50b7b2fbb6255dc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b3a0ae3-c935-558c-a2c5-0ecef7b8a1bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308027} :answer-field/id #uuid "2183b570-2360-54b5-af9a-d3c3dd272f53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1edd034e-43e5-5b4e-a6af-3ada922e1784" :question/math-academy-id "q-184504" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times {1-i}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-0.5,0.5]\\times {0.5}={(k,0.5)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ to the point $(0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/509d3e8d3bce0b422cad7e7f02377468cacc0de3a4e45647c12fef15f2c9991f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times {1-i}={(k,1-i)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(-i,1-i)$ to the point $(i,1-i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/21859c2ac02ba80d47672bf2fab25f7358feb921bc5b546c1e8c783c2819bea3.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0f10b47-a5b1-555f-9a65-f01bd18b2194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/4d714297c6acc363f55a775fb3f6007145dec3aa415476d5c758f8c2def679ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308032} {:answer/id #uuid "72f5ef30-281f-569e-9fb0-36811ba46ad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/86cd6fb9fd52708dd41257a7df95db869ce6638e0dde902e2f7847d09021b530.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308033} {:answer/id #uuid "545f030d-777e-5a51-af82-0d8f610fe650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/204c9a31031534ac70a599322c3f77a077440300fde2364476643c88406e34cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308034} {:answer/id #uuid "53d1f208-7243-51f1-80f1-4a9ab9a8303a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/748c2e450098f81b24d22a34ce375b1ea365c4879a